\section{Why three numbers for one comparison}{
The three estimands answer three different questions about the same two
curves, and each is silent about what the others measure:
\itemize{
\item the \strong{hazard ratio} is one number for the whole of follow-up. It is a
ratio, so it says nothing about how much time the difference is worth,
and it only means what it is usually taken to mean while the hazards stay
proportional. The \code{\link[survival:cox.zph]{survival::cox.zph()}} test of that assumption is
returned in the \code{ph_p} column of every row;
\item the \strong{Kaplan-Meier difference} is the gap between the two curves at one
instant, \code{tau}. It is in percentage points and needs no modelling
assumption, but it uses only the two curve heights at \code{tau} and throws
away everything that happened on the way there;
\item the \strong{RMST difference} is the area between the two curves from 0 to
\code{tau}. It is in months, it needs no proportional-hazards assumption, and
it exists whether or not a median is reached, but it depends on \code{tau} and
averages away the shape of the difference inside the window.
}

Because they measure different things they can, and on real data do,
disagree: a hazard ratio far from 1 can sit next to an RMST difference of a
few weeks, and two estimands can even point at different arms. The columns
below are there to make that disagreement a number rather than an
impression.
}

\section{One analysis set for all three}{
Everything in a row is computed on the same subjects. The analysis set is
built once: the rows whose \code{group} is one of the two \code{arms} are kept, rows
belonging to any other level are counted and left out, and rows with a
missing \code{time}, \code{event} or \code{group} are refused (\code{group_missing = "drop"}
leaves the missing-group rows out instead, uniformly, before any estimator
sees the data).

That set is then handed to \code{\link[=calc_rmst]{calc_rmst()}}, \code{\link[=fit_cox]{fit_cox()}} and \code{\link[=fit_km]{fit_km()}} with
their strictest settings -- \code{group_missing = "error"}, \code{na_action = "fail"},
\code{empty_levels = "error"} -- so none of them can quietly drop a row of its
own accord, and the counts they report back are reconciled against each
other and against the analysis set:
\itemize{
\item rows used, by all three;
\item subjects in each arm, by all three;
\item events in each arm, by all three.
}

Any disagreement is an error, not a warning and not a note. The reconciled
table travels back as \code{attr(x, "compare_estimands")$same_patients}, so the
invariant can be read off the result rather than taken on trust.

A second invariant of the same kind is checked on the estimates themselves:
the Kaplan-Meier survival probabilities used for the difference come from
one \code{survfit(~ 1)} per arm (see below), and they must equal the ones
\code{\link[=fit_km]{fit_km()}} reports at the same \code{tau} to within 1e-10, numbers at risk
included.
}

\section{The tau guard}{
The truncation times are checked by \code{\link[=calc_rmst]{calc_rmst()}}, with the rule it
documents: \code{tau} must not exceed the smaller of the two arms' largest
follow-up times, the boundary is inclusive, and there is no argument that
relaxes it. The guard is not duplicated, not re-implemented and not
bypassed: \code{\link[=calc_rmst]{calc_rmst()}} is called first, before anything else is computed,
so a \code{tau} past the follow-up of the contrast stops the whole call, and the
error message that comes back says \code{calc_rmst()} because that is the
function that refused. The limit is returned as the \code{tau_upper} column.

The same \code{tau} is used for the Kaplan-Meier time points, so the three
columns of a row are truncated at the same place. \code{\link[=fit_km]{fit_km()}} applies the
same rule to its time points, which is why the two can never disagree about
where follow-up ends.
}

\section{What a constant hazard ratio would imply}{
If the hazards really were proportional with ratio \code{theta}, the comparator
arm's survival curve would be \code{S1(t) = S0(t)^theta}. Taking \code{S0} to be the
Kaplan-Meier curve of arm 0 and \code{theta} to be the estimated hazard ratio,
integrating that implied curve from 0 to \code{tau} and subtracting the area
under \code{S0} gives \code{rmst_diff_implied}: the RMST difference one would see at
this \code{tau} if a single hazard ratio told the whole story.

\code{rmst_gap_mo = rmst_diff - rmst_diff_implied} is what is left over -- the
months of time-scale information a single hazard ratio cannot carry. It is
a difference of two areas, never a ratio, so it can be read whatever the
numbers are; \code{gap_lo_mo} and \code{gap_hi_mo} repeat it at the smallest and the
largest \code{tau} on every row.

The integration is the same step-function sum that a Kaplan-Meier area is:
\code{S} is held at \code{S(t_i)} on the interval from \code{t_i} up to but not including
\code{t_i+1}, starting from \code{S(0) = 1}. It is self-checked at every \code{tau}: the
same code applied to \code{S0} itself must reproduce the arm-0 RMST
\code{\link[survRM2:rmst2]{survRM2::rmst2()}} computed independently, to within 1e-6. If it does not,
the integration is wrong, the implied difference is meaningless, and the
call stops rather than returning it.

Two further summaries of the same idea are contrast-level columns, computed
between the smallest and the largest \code{tau} and repeated on every row:
\code{ratio_obs} is \code{d_hi / d_lo}, how much the observed difference grew across
the window, and \code{ratio_implied} is the same ratio for the implied
differences; \code{ratio_dev_pct} is \code{100 * (ratio_obs / ratio_implied - 1)} and
\code{rel_hi_pct} is \code{100 * (d_hi / di_hi - 1)}. A ratio whose denominator sits
against zero is not informative, so a denominator smaller than \code{min_denom}
in absolute value returns \code{NA} with the reason in \code{het_note}, and
\code{gap_hi_mo} is read instead. With a single \code{tau} the smallest and the
largest coincide, \code{ratio_obs} and \code{ratio_implied} are 1 by construction, and
only the gaps are meaningful.
}

\section{The concordance rules}{
The rules are fixed here, in this file and in this section. They do not
depend on the data, no argument tunes them, and nothing in them is chosen
after seeing a result. Read them before reading a \code{verdict}.

\strong{Which arm is worse.} For each estimand, one label, in a column of its
own:
\itemize{
\item \code{dir_hr} is \code{"arm1_worse"} when \code{hr > 1}, else \code{"arm0_worse"};
\item \code{dir_km} is \code{"arm1_worse"} when \code{km_surv_diff < 0}, i.e. arm 1 has the
lower survival probability at \code{tau}, else \code{"arm0_worse"};
\item \code{dir_rmst} is \code{"arm1_worse"} when \code{rmst_diff < 0}, i.e. arm 1 has the
shorter restricted mean survival time, else \code{"arm0_worse"}.
}

The comparisons are strict, so an exact tie -- a hazard ratio of exactly 1,
a difference of exactly 0 -- reads as \code{"arm0_worse"}. Continuous estimates
do not tie in practice. The column \code{dir_km_median} is the same label for the
difference in median survival; it is reported for reference only, is \code{NA}
when a median is not reached, and never enters the judgement.

\strong{Significant or not.} At \code{alpha = 1 - conf_level}, judged from the
confidence interval:
\itemize{
\item \code{sig_hr} : the interval of the hazard ratio excludes 1;
\item \code{sig_km} : the interval of the Kaplan-Meier difference excludes 0;
\item \code{sig_rmst} : the interval of the RMST difference excludes 0.
}

The p-values are returned next to them, and an invariant checks that each
interval gives the same verdict as its own \code{p < alpha}; the interval and the
p-value of each estimand come from the same normal approximation, so they
cannot legitimately differ.

\strong{Do they agree.}
\itemize{
\item \code{direction_agree} : all three direction labels are the same;
\item \code{signif_agree} : all three significance verdicts are the same;
\item \code{n_signif} : how many of the three are significant, 0 to 3;
\item \code{concordant} : \code{direction_agree & signif_agree};
\item \code{inconsistent} : \code{!concordant}.
}

\strong{What kind of disagreement.} \code{discordance_type} takes exactly one of four
values, tested in this order:
\enumerate{
\item \code{"concordant"} -- \code{concordant} is \code{TRUE};
\item \code{"direction_conflict_significant"} -- the directions differ \strong{and} some
pair of estimands that point at different arms are \strong{both} significant.
That pair contradicts each other on the data's own terms, and this is the
row a Results section has to address;
\item \code{"direction_conflict_ci_covers_null"} -- the directions differ, but no
such pair is both significant: every sign flip involves an estimand whose
interval covers the null. A difference sitting against zero has a sign,
and that sign is noise, so this is \strong{not} counted as a contradiction;
\item \code{"significance_only"} -- the directions all agree and only the
significance verdicts differ.
}

The pair test is the column \code{dir_conflict_signif}, which is \code{TRUE} when
\code{(sig_hr & sig_km & dir_hr != dir_km)} or
\code{(sig_hr & sig_rmst & dir_hr != dir_rmst)} or
\code{(sig_km & sig_rmst & dir_km != dir_rmst)}.

The boundary case is settled by rule 3 and is worth stating on its own,
because it decides how many contradictions a table appears to contain:
\strong{two estimands that point at different arms while at least one of the two
has an interval covering the null are not a contradiction.} Such a row is
still \code{inconsistent} -- the directions really do differ -- but it is typed
apart from a real conflict and its \code{verdict} begins with \verb{minor:} rather
than \code{DISCORDANT}. Counting sign flips near zero as findings would inflate
the disagreement the function exists to measure.

\strong{The sentence.} \code{sig_pattern} is \code{"HR sig/ns / KM sig/ns / RMST sig/ns"},
and \code{verdict} is one of
\itemize{
\item \code{"concordant: all three significant, same direction"};
\item \code{"concordant: all three non-significant, same direction"};
\item \code{"DISCORDANT (direction): two significant estimands point to different arms (<sig_pattern>)"};
\item \code{"minor: directions differ but the conflicting estimands are not both significant (<sig_pattern>)"};
\item \code{"DISCORDANT (significance): <n> of 3 significant, same direction (<sig_pattern>)"}.
}

A concordant row always has \code{n_signif} equal to 0 or 3, since \code{signif_agree}
is part of \code{concordant}; that is checked rather than assumed.
}

\section{Adjustment}{
All three estimands are unadjusted. That is deliberate and it is what makes
the comparison a comparison of estimands: a multivariable hazard ratio put
next to an unadjusted RMST difference would differ from it partly because of
covariate adjustment, and there would be no way to tell which part is which.
If an adjusted hazard ratio is wanted alongside, fit it with \code{\link[=fit_cox]{fit_cox()}} and
add it as a reference column of your own; it does not belong in the
concordance judgement.
}

\section{Input checks}{
The call stops, with the offending columns, values or counts listed, when
\enumerate{
\item a required column (\code{time}, \code{event}, \code{group}) is not in \code{data}, appears in
\code{data} more than once, or two of the three name the same column;
\item \code{time} is not numeric, or holds missing, negative or infinite values;
\item \code{event} holds a value other than \code{0} and \code{1}, missing values included;
\item \code{group} has fewer than two observed levels, or has more than two and
\code{arms} was not given, or \code{arms} does not name exactly two levels that
occur in the data;
\item \code{group} has missing values and \code{group_missing = "error"} (the default);
\item \code{tau} is not a positive, finite, non-missing, non-duplicated numeric
vector;
\item any element of \code{tau} is past the smallest of the two arms' largest
follow-up times. This one is raised by \code{\link[=calc_rmst]{calc_rmst()}} and says so;
\item an arm has no rows, or no event anywhere in follow-up. Also
\code{\link[=calc_rmst]{calc_rmst()}}'s;
\item \code{conf_level}, \code{ph_alpha}, \code{min_denom} or any \code{digits_} argument is not a
single value of the right kind;
\item the three estimators do not report the same subjects and events, or the
Kaplan-Meier probabilities from the two routes disagree, or the
step-function integral does not reproduce survRM2's arm-0 RMST, or the
assembled table fails one of its own identities. None of these can be
caused by an ordinary input; they are reported as bugs.
}
}

\examples{
## A small simulated cohort. No real patient records are used anywhere in
## this package.
set.seed(20260901)
site <- factor(rep(c("Stomach", "Small intestine", "Colorectal"), each = 80),
               levels = c("Stomach", "Small intestine", "Colorectal"))
rate <- c(Stomach = 0.010, "Small intestine" = 0.018,
          Colorectal = 0.030)[as.character(site)]
t_event  <- stats::rexp(240, rate)
t_censor <- stats::runif(240, 24, 140)
toy <- data.frame(
  site     = site,
  time_mo  = round(pmin(t_event, t_censor), 1),
  event_os = as.integer(t_event <= t_censor)
)

## The three estimands side by side, one row per truncation time.
ce <- compare_estimands(toy, "site", arms = c("Stomach", "Colorectal"))
ce[, c("comparison", "tau", "hr_txt", "km_surv_diff_txt", "rmst_diff_txt")]

## One hazard ratio stands for the whole of follow-up; the other two do not.
## The proportional-hazards test travels on every row.
ce[, c("tau", "hr", "hr_p_fmt", "ph_p_fmt", "ph_violated")]

## The concordance judgement, by the rules in the Details section.
ce[, c("tau", "dir_hr", "dir_km", "dir_rmst", "sig_pattern",
       "direction_agree", "signif_agree", "discordance_type")]
ce$verdict

## What a constant hazard ratio would have implied for the RMST difference,
## and how many months the observed difference misses it by.
ce[, c("tau", "rmst_diff", "rmst_diff_implied", "rmst_gap_mo")]

## Every column the table carries.
names(ce)

## The diagnostics. same_patients is the invariant that makes the row a
## comparison of estimands rather than of analysis sets.
d <- attr(ce, "compare_estimands")
d$counts
d$follow_up
d$same_patients
d$guard
d$checks

## The concordance rules come back with the result, so a verdict never has
## to be taken on trust.
d$rules$direction
d$rules$significance
d$rules$discordance_type
d$rules$boundary_case

## The rules earn their keep when the curves cross. A scenario built so
## that arm B is harmed early and helped later: one hazard ratio stands for
## both halves and fails its own assumption, the Kaplan-Meier difference
## changes sign between 12 and 36 months, and the RMST difference needs a
## long window before it becomes significant.
set.seed(20260901)
n     <- 150
early <- stats::rexp(n, 0.055)
late  <- 30 + stats::rexp(n, 0.004)
t_b   <- ifelse(stats::runif(n) < 0.35, early, late)
t_a   <- stats::rexp(n, 0.020)
cens  <- stats::runif(2 * n, 36, 120)
cross <- data.frame(trt = factor(rep(c("A", "B"), each = n)),
                    raw = c(t_a, t_b))
cross$time_mo  <- round(pmin(cross$raw, cens), 1)
cross$event_os <- as.integer(cross$raw <= cens)
cc <- compare_estimands(cross, "trt")
cc[, c("tau", "hr_txt", "km_surv_diff_txt", "rmst_diff_txt", "ph_p_fmt")]

## Three truncation times, three different verdicts, all by the rules
## above: a sign flip whose intervals cover the null is typed apart from a
## real conflict, and never called one.
cc[, c("tau", "dir_hr", "dir_km", "dir_rmst", "sig_pattern",
       "discordance_type")]
cc$verdict

## The same hazard ratio implies a much larger RMST difference than the one
## observed, at every tau. That gap, in months, is what the single number
## cannot carry.
cc[, c("tau", "rmst_diff", "rmst_diff_implied", "rmst_gap_mo")]

## A cohort followed for at most 30 months: the tau guard refuses the same
## call. It is calc_rmst()'s guard, not a copy of it, and the message says
## so.
recent <- toy
recent$event_os[recent$time_mo > 30] <- 0L
recent$time_mo <- pmin(recent$time_mo, 30)
try(compare_estimands(recent, "site", arms = c("Stomach", "Colorectal")))

## Rows with a missing time, event or group are refused rather than dropped
## by each estimator on its own terms: three columns built on three
## different subsets would not be a comparison of estimands.
gappy <- toy
gappy$site[c(1, 2, 3)] <- NA
try(compare_estimands(gappy, "site", arms = c("Stomach", "Colorectal")))

## Three levels and no `arms`: which two were meant is not guessed.
try(compare_estimands(toy, "site"))

}
